<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Scandinavia and the Baltic</p><h1 class="st-title">Faroese Commonwealth</h1><p class="st-meta">825 CE – 1035 CE (approx.) · capital Tinganes</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Self-governing Norse community of the Faroe Islands, ruled by its assembly at Tinganes until it came under the Norwegian crown.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Norse settlers reached the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/faroe-islands">Faroe Islands</a> in the early 9th century; the Irish monk <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dicuil">Dicuil</a> wrote around 825 that Irish hermits there had been driven out by Northmen raiders. The settlers were farmers and herders, many from western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a>, and some with Gaelic links from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-isles">British Isles</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The islanders governed themselves through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/logting">Alting</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tinganes">Tinganes</a>, in what is now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/torshavn">Tórshavn</a>, one of the oldest parliamentary sites in the world. Sheep farming, fowling and fishing supported the population. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/faereyinga-saga">Færeyinga saga</a> tells how <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sigmundur-brestisson">Sigmundur Brestisson</a> brought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> around 999 for King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olaf-tryggvason">Olaf Tryggvason</a>, opposed by the chieftain <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trondur-i-gotu">Tróndur í Gøtu</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>By about 1035 the islands paid tax to the Norwegian king and became part of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-norway">Kingdom of Norway</a>. They later passed to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/denmark-norway">Denmark-Norway</a> and remained with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> after 1814. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/logting">Løgting</a> still meets in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/torshavn">Tórshavn</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/faroe-islands">Faroe Islands</a> are a self-governing part of the Danish realm.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="other, 825 CE – 1035 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><ellipse cx="44" cy="56" rx="16" ry="24"/><path d="M44 34 V78"/><path d="M68 20 V84"/><path d="M68 20 L63 30 H73 Z"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">other</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Scandinavia and the Baltic</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:76.12%;width:4.18%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>825 CE – 1035 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Tinganes</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Sigmundur Brestisson, Tróndur í Gøtu</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Norse paganism, later Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Old Norse</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/denmark">Denmark</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Viking Age Faroe Islands, Faroese Løgting realm</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=930&amp;focus=faroese-commonwealth" aria-label="Faroese Commonwealth on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4541 118 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Faroese Commonwealth lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M4861 374L5225 425"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5225 425h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4861 374h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4861 374h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Tinganes. <a href="/atlas?year=930&amp;focus=faroese-commonwealth">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
